tel 1.0

base tessellate
import soundness

info   text layout in fixed-width cells: wrapping, fitting, alignment, track sizing and box-drawing characters
detail
    _Tessellate_ is the layout layer shared by Soundness's terminal and table libraries. It
    arranges textual content — `Text`, `Teletype`, or any `Textual` whose elements are `Char`s —
    into a grid of fixed-width cells, measuring every line by its display width rather than its
    length, so East Asian wide characters, combining marks and joiner-glued emoji occupy the
    cells they really do.

    Its pieces are small and independent:

    - `Flow` flows a paragraph into lines (`Flow.wrap`, breaking at hyphenation points, then at
      spaces), confines one line to a width (`Flow.fit`, truncating with an ellipsis or padding),
      and measures content's intrinsic widths (`Flow.metrics`);
    - `Alignment` pads a line to a width: `Left`, `Right`, `Center` or `Justify`;
    - `Metrics` and `Flex` describe tracks — columns, rows, panes — competing for space along
      an axis, and `Flex.solve` shares the space out among them;
    - `Reflowable` is the typeclass of content which negotiates with a rectangular layout;
    - `BoxLine`, `LineCharset` and `BoxDrawing` choose the box-drawing character for a junction
      of lines.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
    import textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric

    Flow.wrap(t"the quick brown fox", 10)          // the quick, brown fox
    Flow.fit(t"hello world", 8)                    // t"hello w…"
    Flow.fit(t"abc", 6, Alignment.Right)           // t"   abc"
    Flow.metrics(t"the quick brown\nfox")          // Metrics(5, 15)
    ```

    Every measuring method needs a `Text is Measurable` in scope, which comes from a
    `Char is Measurable` chosen by name from the `textMetrics` package (`uniformMetric`,
    `eastAsianScriptsMetric` or `wideCharacterWidthMetric`). Wrapping needs a `Hyphenation` as
    well: with none imported, the companion's fallback hyphenates nothing, and importing
    `hyphenations.englishHyphenation` enables American-English hyphenation. Both are imported by
    name, since `import soundness.*` does not import givens.

    Positions within content are char offsets into its plain text, so the content's own
    indexing must agree with its plain text's chars, which is true of `Text`, `Ascii` and
    `Teletype`.

    The library has one source set, `tessellate.core`, which compiles for every platform and
    is exported in full to the `soundness` umbrella. It is the layout engine beneath
    escritoire's tables and ultimatum's frames and borders.

refer tessellate.Flow
refer tessellate.Alignment
refer tessellate.Flex
refer tessellate.Reflowable

entry .Flow   wraps, fits, truncates, chops and measures textual content within a width of cells
  detail
      `Flow`'s methods work on any `textual` whose `Textual` instance has `Char` elements —
      `Text`, `Teletype` — and return the same type, so styled text keeps its styles across the
      lines it is broken into. Every width is measured by grapheme cluster under the
      `Text is Measurable` in scope: a cluster is as wide as its widest codepoint, so a
      combining mark rides on its base character, and a flag or a ZWJ emoji family counts
      once.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
      import textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric

      Flow.wrap(t"hyphenation", 7)         // t"hyphen-", t"ation"
      Flow.wrap(t"日本語 テスト", 6)        // t"日本語", t"テスト"
      Flow.fit(t"日本語abc", 5)            // t"日本…"
      Flow.chop(t"abcdefgh", 3)            // t"abc", t"def", t"gh"
      ```
  refer tessellate.Alignment
  refer tessellate.Metrics
  entry .wrap   flows content into lines no wider than a width, hyphenating and breaking at spaces
    detail
        `Flow.wrap(content, width)` returns a `Sequence` of lines. A hard break (`\n` or `\r\n`)
        always starts a new line, and consecutive hard breaks keep the empty line between them.
        Where a soft break is needed, the latest hyphenation point of the overflowing word which
        still fits with `hyphen` (by default `t"-"`) appended is preferred; failing that, the line
        breaks at its last space; failing that, the over-long word runs on beyond the width. A
        width below 1 returns the content as a single line.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label No hyphenation in scope: the companion's fallback
          code
              import soundness.*
              import textMetrics.uniformMetric

              Flow.wrap(t"a hyphenation", 9)   // t"a", t"hyphenation"
        frame
          label English hyphenation imported by name
          code
              import soundness.*
              import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
              import textMetrics.uniformMetric

              Flow.wrap(t"a hyphenation", 9)   // t"a hyphen-", t"ation"
        ##
        ```

        The spaces a soft break consumes are dropped, but trailing spaces at a hard break or at
        the end of the content are content — a styled badge such as `e"$Bg(green)( ✓ )"` needs
        all three cells — and are clipped only as far as the width, since padding never
        truncates.

        `unbreakable` names spans of the content (as `Interval`s of char offsets into its plain
        text) which must survive intact, such as a verbatim code sample amid prose. A span's
        spaces are not break opportunities and its hyphenation points are ignored, so it wraps
        as one word, moving whole to the next line or running on beyond the width; a space at a
        span's edge is still a break opportunity, and a hard break inside one still ends the
        line.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
        import textMetrics.uniformMetric

        Flow.wrap(t"see foo bar baz here", 12, unbreakable = List(4.z thru 14.u))
        // t"see", t"foo bar baz", t"here"
        ```

        It needs a `Text is Measurable` and a `Hyphenation` in scope.
  entry .fit   confines one line to exactly a width: truncated with an ellipsis, or padded by alignment
      `Flow.fit(content, width, alignment, ellipsis, last)` shortens content wider than the
      width with `.shorten`, and pads anything narrower with `alignment` (`Alignment.Left` by
      default). `last` is passed to the alignment, and tells `Alignment.Justify` whether the line
      ends its paragraph.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      Flow.fit(t"abc", 6)                       // t"abc   "
      Flow.fit(t"ab", 6, Alignment.Center)      // t"  ab  "
      Flow.fit(t"hello world", 8)               // t"hello w…"
      ```
  entry .shorten   truncates content wider than a width, marking the cut with an ellipsis
      Content which already fits is returned unchanged. Otherwise as many whole grapheme
      clusters are kept as fit with `ellipsis` (by default `t"…"`) appended, so a wide
      character never straddles the cut. The result may be narrower than the width; `.fit`
      pads it.
  entry .chop   cuts content into lines of at most a width, ignoring word boundaries
      Character flow, by grapheme cluster, as a terminal itself wraps a long line. A width
      below 1, or empty content, gives the content as one line.
  entry .minContent   the narrowest width content can wrap into without overflow: its widest word
      The display width of its widest word, where words are delimited by spaces and hard
      breaks.
  entry .natural   the width content occupies unwrapped: its widest hard line
  entry .metrics   both intrinsic widths of content, `.minContent` and `.natural`, as `Metrics`
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric

      Flow.metrics(t"the quick brown\nfox")   // Metrics(5, 15)
      Flow.metrics(t"👨‍👩‍👧 ab")              // Metrics(2, 5)
      ```

entry .Alignment   horizontal placement of a line within a wider extent: left, right, centre or justified
  detail
      An `Alignment` pads content to exactly a width with `#pad`; it never truncates, so content
      wider than the width is returned as it is (`Flow.fit` truncates first).

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Left: padded on the right
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric

            Alignment.Left.pad(t"a b c", 9)   // t"a b c    "
      frame
        label Right: padded on the left
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric

            Alignment.Right.pad(t"a b c", 9)   // t"    a b c"
      frame
        label Center: odd space on the right
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric

            Alignment.Center.pad(t"a b c", 9)   // t"  a b c  "
      frame
        label Justify: word gaps stretched, except on a paragraph's last line
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric

            Alignment.Justify.pad(t"a b c", 9, last = false)   // t"a   b   c"
      ##
      ```
  refer tessellate.Flow
  entry .Left   pads content on the right
  entry .Right   pads content on the left
  entry .Center   centres content, putting an odd cell of spare space on the right
      Unlike gossamer's `center`, which puts the odd cell on the left.
  entry .Justify   stretches the gaps between words to fill the width, except on a paragraph's last line
      When `last` is true, the line is padded on the right, as `Left` pads it: a paragraph's
      final line is not stretched. Otherwise the spare space is shared among the word gaps,
      with any remainder going to the later gaps.
  entry .Vertical   vertical placement of content within a taller extent: `Top`, `Middle` or `Bottom`
      An enumeration of three cases, used where a cell is shorter than the row it sits in —
      when another cell of the row wraps onto more lines.
  entry #pad   pads content to exactly a width, given whether it is a paragraph's last line
      `pad(content, width, last)`, with `last` true by default; only `Justify` heeds it. It
      needs a `Text is Measurable` in scope.

entry .Metrics   the intrinsic widths of content along one axis: min-content and natural
  detail
      `Metrics(min, natural)`: `min` is the narrowest extent the content can be arranged into
      without overflowing (its widest word, for text), and `natural` the extent it takes given
      unlimited space (its widest line). `Flow.metrics` measures text, and `Reflowable#metrics`
      any reflowable content.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Metrics(5, 15).max(Metrics(8, 10))   // Metrics(8, 15)
      Metrics(4)                           // Metrics(4, 4)
      ```
  refer tessellate.Flex
  entry .apply   metrics of a fixed width, whose minimum and natural widths are equal
  entry #min   the narrowest extent the content fits without overflow
  entry #natural   the extent the content occupies given unlimited space
  entry #max   the larger of two metrics, minimum by minimum and natural by natural
      The metrics of a column holding both contents.

entry .Flex   a track competing for space along an axis: its metrics, weight, bound and collapse behaviour
  detail
      A `Flex` describes one track — a column, a row, a pane — to `Flex.solve`: its content's
      `#metrics`, the `#weight` with which it claims spare space (`1.0` by default), an optional
      upper bound `#max`, and whether it is `#collapsible`: dropped entirely, lowest `#rank`
      first, when the axis cannot hold every track's minimum.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val tracks = Sequence(Flex(Metrics(5)), Flex(Metrics(5), collapsible = true))
      Flex.solve(tracks, 6)   // 6, Unset
      ```
  refer tessellate.Metrics
  entry .content   a track whose bounds derive from its content alone
      It shrinks to its minimum, never grows beyond its natural width, and claims spare space
      in proportion to how much it can usefully absorb, the difference between the two.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Flex.solve(Sequence(Flex.content(Metrics(3, 9)), Flex.content(Metrics(2, 4))), 20)   // 9, 4
      ```
  entry .solve   shares an axis's cells among tracks, giving each track's size or `Unset` if it collapsed
    detail
        `Flex.solve(tracks, available, gap)` works in two stages.

        **Collapse.** While the tracks' minima, plus a `gap` between each pair, do not fit in
        `available`, the collapsible track of lowest rank (the leftmost, on a tie) is dropped,
        and its size is `Unset`. If nothing more can collapse, the minima are kept and overflow
        the space, and the caller decides what to do.

        **Distribute.** Each remaining track's fair share of the space left after the gaps is
        in proportion to its weight. A track whose share would fall below its minimum is pinned
        at the minimum, and one whose share would exceed its `max` is pinned at the maximum,
        with the rest redistributed among the others until nothing changes. The free tracks
        then take their shares with largest-remainder rounding, so the sizes sum to exactly the
        space available.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Flex.solve(Sequence(Flex(Metrics(0)), Flex(Metrics(0)), Flex(Metrics(0))), 10)
        // three sizes summing to 10

        Flex.solve(Sequence(Flex(Metrics(6, 10)), Flex(Metrics(0, 10))), 8)   // 6, 2
        Flex.solve(Sequence(Flex(Metrics(0), max = 2), Flex(Metrics(0))), 10)  // 2, 8
        Flex.solve(Sequence(Flex(Metrics(0)), Flex(Metrics(0))), 10, gap = 2)  // 4, 4
        ```
  entry #metrics   the track content's intrinsic widths; its minimum is a lower bound
  entry #weight   the proportion of spare space the track claims, relative to the others
  entry #max   a hard upper bound on the track's size, or `Unset` for none
  entry #rank   the track's collapse order: among collapsible tracks, the lowest rank is dropped first
  entry #collapsible   whether the track may be dropped entirely when the minima cannot all fit

entry .Reflowable   content which negotiates with a rectangular layout: its widths, its height, its lines
  detail
      A `Reflowable` reports the intrinsic widths its content wants (`#metrics`), how many lines
      it takes at a candidate width (`#height`), and finally arranges itself into lines of
      exactly the settled width (`#flow`). Its `#Line` is the type of a rendered line: for
      self-rendering content such as `Text` or `Teletype`, the content type itself.

      Every `Textual` with `Char` elements is reflowable through `.textual`, in implicit scope,
      by wrapping with `Flow.wrap` and padding each line with the alignment.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      summon[Text is Reflowable].metrics(t"one two")   // Metrics(3, 7)
      summon[Text is Reflowable].flow(t"the quick brown fox", 10, Alignment.Left)
      // t"the quick ", t"brown fox "
      ```

      escritoire's table cells and ultimatum's panes take their content through this
      typeclass.
  refer tessellate.Flow
  refer tessellate.Metrics
  entry .textual   makes any `Char`-element `Textual` reflowable, its lines of its own type
      Metrics come from `Flow.metrics`; lines come from `Flow.wrap`, each padded to the width
      with the alignment, the final line being treated as its paragraph's last.
  entry #Line   the type of one rendered line of the content
  entry #metrics   the content's intrinsic widths, under the `Text is Measurable` in scope
  entry #height   how many lines the content takes at a width
      By default, the number of lines `#flow` produces at that width, aligned left.
  entry #flow   the content arranged into lines of exactly a width, aligned as given
      Needs a `Text is Measurable` and a `Hyphenation` in scope.

entry .BoxLine   the weight of one arm of a box-drawing junction: `Blank`, `Thin`, `Thick` or `Double`
    An enumeration of four cases. A box-drawing character is chosen by the weights of its four
    arms — up, right, down and left — through `LineCharset#apply`, so `Blank` on every arm is
    a space, and `Thin` above and below with `Blank` either side is a vertical line.

entry .LineCharset   a family of box-drawing characters: `Default`, `Rounded` or `Ascii`
  detail
      `Default` is the Unicode box-drawing set, with thin, thick and double lines and the
      junctions between them; `Rounded` is the same with rounded corners for the four thin
      corners; and `Ascii` approximates every character with `-`, `|` and `+`, for a terminal
      with no Unicode.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      LineCharset.Default(top = BoxLine.Thin, bottom = BoxLine.Thin)            // a vertical line
      LineCharset.Rounded(right = BoxLine.Thin, bottom = BoxLine.Thin)          // a rounded corner
      LineCharset.Ascii(BoxLine.Thin, BoxLine.Thin, BoxLine.Thin, BoxLine.Thin) // '+'
      ```
  refer tessellate.BoxLine
  refer tessellate.BoxDrawing
  entry #apply   the junction character for four arms, or the whole table of characters
      `charset(top, right, bottom, left)` gives the character whose arms have those
      `BoxLine` weights, each `Blank` by default. `charset()` gives the charset's whole table,
      as a frozen array of 256 characters indexed by the four weights.

entry .BoxDrawing   the tables of box-drawing characters behind `LineCharset`
  detail
      Each table is a frozen array of 256 characters, indexed by the weights of a junction's
      four arms: `left + 4*bottom + 16*right + 64*top`, each the `ordinal` of a `BoxLine`. A
      combination with no character of its own has the nearest one available.
  refer tessellate.LineCharset
  entry .asciiChars   the table for `LineCharset.Ascii`: every junction as `-`, `|`, `+` or a space
  entry .defaultChars   the table for `LineCharset.Default`: the Unicode box-drawing characters
  entry .roundedChars   the table for `LineCharset.Rounded`: as the default, with rounded thin corners
  entry .simple   the character for a straight line or a crossing, from one vertical and one horizontal weight
      `BoxDrawing.simple(vertical, horizontal, charset)` is
      `charset(vertical, horizontal, vertical, horizontal)`: the vertical weight above and
      below, and the horizontal weight on either side.
